WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.985 --> 00:00:01.352
Rebecca Robbins: All right.

00:00:01.352 --> 00:00:04.154
"Many adults need five
hours of sleep or less."

00:00:04.154 --> 00:00:05.756
Now, this is a myth.

00:00:05.756 --> 00:00:08.625
"Loud snoring is annoying
but mostly harmless."

00:00:08.625 --> 00:00:11.695
David Rapoport: Loud
snoring is actually a sign

00:00:11.695 --> 00:00:14.198
that there is a
blockage in your throat.

00:00:14.198 --> 00:00:15.199
Robbins: "Your brain
and body

00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:16.761
will adapt to less sleep."

00:00:16.761 --> 00:00:18.102
This is a myth.

00:00:18.102 --> 00:00:19.333
I'm Dr. Rebecca Robbins.

00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:20.771
I'm a postdoctoral
research fellow

00:00:20.771 --> 00:00:21.975
at the Brigham and
Women's Hospital

00:00:21.975 --> 00:00:23.191
and Harvard
Medical School.

00:00:23.191 --> 00:00:24.802
Rapoport: And I'm
Dr. David Rapoport.

00:00:24.802 --> 00:00:26.314
I'm a professor of medicine

00:00:26.314 --> 00:00:29.055
at the Icahn School of
Medicine at Mount Sinai,

00:00:29.055 --> 00:00:31.131
and I run the research
program in sleep.

00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:32.611
Robbins: And we're here
today to debunk

00:00:32.611 --> 00:00:34.683
some of the most common
myths about our sleep.

00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:36.153
"Watching TV in bed is a

00:00:36.153 --> 00:00:38.222
good way to relax
before sleep."

00:00:38.222 --> 00:00:39.757
Now, this is not something

00:00:39.757 --> 00:00:41.592
that we would
necessarily recommend.

00:00:41.592 --> 00:00:44.328
If you turn the television
on and if it's close to you,

00:00:44.328 --> 00:00:45.796
that's a source of
bright-blue light.

00:00:45.796 --> 00:00:47.898
So, bright light is one
of the strongest cues

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:49.860
to our circadian rhythm.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.660
It kick-starts our
body and our brain

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.260
to become awake and
alert in the morning.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:55.830
It's called a zeitgeber,

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.908
the strongest input
to that circadian,

00:00:57.908 --> 00:00:59.810
the awake phase
of our rhythm.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.425
Rapoport: "Drinking
booze before bed

00:01:02.425 --> 00:01:03.616
will improve your sleep."

00:01:03.616 --> 00:01:06.610
So, this is a very
commonly used tactic

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:09.127
for people who have
trouble sleeping,

00:01:09.127 --> 00:01:10.551
and they have a drink.

00:01:10.551 --> 00:01:11.560
It's a drug.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.800
It's very much like
a sleeping pill.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.330
And it is true that it
will help you get to sleep,

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:18.760
as long as you don't
drink too much.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.390
One or two drinks, perhaps.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:23.790
What you do, however, is it
disrupts the normal sleep.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:25.480
It suppresses REM sleep,

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.400
which is a normal
part of your sleep

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.630
that comes on a little
while after you go to sleep,

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:31.610
typically 30 to 60
minutes later.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:33.335
And then, when it comes

00:01:33.335 --> 00:01:36.060
that the alcohol has
gotten out of your system,

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.640
then the REM comes back
perhaps at the wrong time,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.710
perhaps too strong,
and it disrupts things.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.430
And so basically it is
not generally recommended

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.730
that alcohol be used
as a sleeping pill.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:49.620
"Lying in bed with
your eyes closed

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.870
is almost as
good as sleeping."

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.350
I think that one's pretty
definitely not correct.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.660
Sleep is a very
specific process

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.160
that your body
goes through.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.790
The most common
myth, if you will,

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:05.980
that we got rid of in the
scientific field 50 years ago

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.960
is that sleep was like,
you know, taking your car

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.560
and putting it in the garage
and turning off the key

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:10.751
and leaving it there,

00:02:10.751 --> 00:02:12.360
and then you come
back the next morning

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.820
and it just is parked.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:15.250
Sleep is not like that at all.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.150
Sleep is a very
active process.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.210
When you go to sleep,
you enter one stage.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.500
A little while later,
you enter another stage.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.080
It gets
progressively deeper.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.440
You then have
the REM sleep,

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:26.360
and then you wake
up momentarily,

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:27.990
and that whole cycle
takes an hour

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.340
to an hour and a half,
and then it starts again,

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.540
and it happens three to
five times in a night.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.290
And if you disrupt
any of that,

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:37.570
something happens, and the
next morning you feel it.

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.260
You don't feel rested.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.040
Now, we don't understand
how that actually happens

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.950
or why that happens, but
we do know it does happen.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:47.700
So when you're lying in bed,
none of that is happening.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.220
If your eyes are closed
and you're not asleep,

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.680
it just doesn't count.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.438
Robbins: Next.

00:02:53.438 --> 00:02:55.570
Rapoport: "If you can't
sleep, you should stay in bed

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:57.350
and try to fall back asleep."

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.840
If you don't fall asleep,

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.700
we generally recommend
that you not stretch it out

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.260
and stress yourself out
by just trying.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.236
And there's
probably nothing

00:03:06.236 --> 00:03:07.730
that can prevent sleep

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.665
as well as,
"I've gotta go to sleep."

00:03:10.665 --> 00:03:12.950
[Robbins laughs]
"I've gotta go to sleep."

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:14.357
"I've gotta go to sleep."
Robbins: "I need to!"

00:03:14.357 --> 00:03:15.332
Rapoport: You can feel
your pulse

00:03:15.332 --> 00:03:16.770
and your blood
pressure going up.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.348
So what we try
and do when

00:03:18.348 --> 00:03:19.360
 we work with
somebody

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.540
who has this problem
with insomnia

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.630
is exactly the
opposite of that.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:26.460
We try and tell them,
relax, don't worry about it.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.830
Stay in bed for a little
while and see what happens.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.650
But don't try to go
to sleep, just relax.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:34.230
And if you can't relax and
if you don't go to sleep,

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.780
it's probably better
to get up

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.000
so that you don't
associate the bed

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.200
with a stressful situation.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.433
Robbins: All right.
"Many adults need

00:03:41.433 --> 00:03:43.358
five hours of sleep or less."

00:03:43.358 --> 00:03:44.960
Now, this is a myth.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:47.177
We have scores of
epidemiological data

00:03:47.177 --> 00:03:49.400
and data from the
sleep lab to show

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.980
that five hours
is not enough

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:53.880
for the vast
majority of adults.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.180
There may be
some individuals

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.760
that maybe do
OK on six hours,

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.930
but much less than that
really is a myth.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:02.240
Now, you might hear
people brag about this,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.590
saying, "Oh I get five,
I'm just fine."

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:08.010
But by and large, we do
see those people

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.780
likely making up for lost
sleep on the weekends

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.440
or in power naps,
for instance.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.020
So, for the vast
majority of us,

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:18.970
the recommendation really
is seven to eight hours.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:20.527
Rapoport: This is a
real problem

00:04:20.527 --> 00:04:22.890
that the sleep field has
been trying to address,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:26.420
and that is that not
sleeping has been perceived

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:28.230
as a macho thing.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:30.910
It proves how great you are,

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:33.080
it proves how manly
you are in some cases.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.927
Sleeping is actually good,

00:04:34.927 --> 00:04:37.200
and you should sort of
be proud of the fact

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.790
that you sleep to your need.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.603
Robbins: "Your brain and body
will adapt to less sleep."

00:04:42.756 --> 00:04:43.931
Rapoport: That sounds
like yours.

00:04:43.931 --> 00:04:44.839
Robbins: No.

00:04:44.839 --> 00:04:46.250
This is a myth.

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:48.310
We see that, just
like good nutrition

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.060
or a great, healthy diet
is so important,

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.350
we similarly have a
diet that we need

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.800
our brains and our bodies
to be at their best.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.581
Rapoport: There are actual,
formal studies

00:04:57.581 --> 00:04:58.460
that have tested

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:01.670
how people perform
with lack of sleep

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.260
and how they think
they are performing.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.880
And it turns out that we
basically are really lousy

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.640
at saying how
sleepy we are.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:11.928
So you know you feel bad

00:05:11.928 --> 00:05:13.260
when you haven't
had enough sleep,

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.510
but you have no idea
how bad you are,

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.870
and your performance
keeps deteriorating

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:19.300
the more you don't sleep

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.250
or restrict your sleep
over multiple days,

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:24.337
and you think, "Oh,
I've settled in.

00:05:24.337 --> 00:05:26.287
I have a little headache, and
it doesn't really bother me.

00:05:26.287 --> 00:05:27.680
I'm doing just great."

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:29.100
And what is actually
happening is

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:30.840
you're performing less
and less well

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:32.620
on the various things
that we can test,

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.050
including driving simulators.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.150
You're falling asleep
for three or four seconds

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.738
continuously, without
knowing it.

00:05:38.738 --> 00:05:39.571
Robbins: All right. "It
doesn't matter

00:05:39.571 --> 00:05:41.296
what time of day you sleep."

00:05:41.296 --> 00:05:43.670
Rapoport: If you look
at our biology,

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:47.000
we have, inside
our brain, a clock.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.250
That clock is set to say,

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:51.170
"This is a good
time to sleep."

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:52.880
And then at another
time it says,

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.500
"This is a good 
time to be out."

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:55.970
Sleep is timed.

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:57.800
It doesn't just happen.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.500
And even if you don't
sleep for the whole night,

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.110
you'll be more and more
sleepy all night long.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:05.110
But in the morning,
you'll get a second wind,

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:07.620
and that's because the clock
says, "Up, time to be up."

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.052
It doesn't matter that you
didn't sleep, 

00:06:09.052 --> 00:06:09.920
it's time to be up.

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:14.340
As Rebecca said, we've
gained an incredible ability

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.240
to not abide by that rhythm.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.100
And the problem is
that people think

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.070
that they can get
away with things

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.310
that our biology
just won't let us do.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:24.200
Nurses have been
most studied for this,

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:27.310
and firefighters and
emergency workers

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:29.400
and people who
live on ships.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.680
They all pay a price,
epidemiologically.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.390
We've shown higher
heart disease,

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:34.880
more tendency to
gain weight,

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.430
and a variety of
malfunctions and difficulties

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:39.510
as time goes on.

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:42.020
You can do it, but
it's gonna cost you.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:44.070
Robbins: "Exercising within
four hours of bedtime

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:45.340
will disturb your sleep."

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:46.881
Rapoport: What we give
as advice is that

00:06:46.881 --> 00:06:48.556
about an hour before sleep,

00:06:48.556 --> 00:06:51.960
you wanna try and avoid
active kind of things,

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:53.752
and exercise certainly
is one of them.

00:06:53.752 --> 00:06:56.350
On the other hand, there
are people who exercise

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:58.860
close to sleep and
do very well.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.760
So I don't think we
should say, you know,

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:02.706
if you're somebody who

00:07:02.706 --> 00:07:04.270
exercises regularly
in the evening

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:05.480
and you sleep beautifully

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:07.480
and you feel rested
the next morning,

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.200
that you should
give up exercise,

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.540
'cause I think that would
be a bad bit of advice.

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:12.373
You'll gain weight,

00:07:12.373 --> 00:07:14.320
you'll lose the toning
that you've gotten,

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:15.460
so on and so forth.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.170
On the other hand, if you
haven't been doing it,

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:20.323
I probably wouldn't start
exercising at night.

00:07:20.323 --> 00:07:21.950
And if you're having
trouble with sleep,

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:23.830
that's one of the
first things we look at,

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:26.930
after drugs like caffeine,
to try and get rid of.

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:28.690
Robbins: All right.
"Remembering your dreams

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:30.270
is a sign of a
good night's sleep."

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:32.920
Rapoport: I think that
there is a huge variation

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:35.830
in how much people
remember their dreams.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.740
Some of it has to do
with when you wake up.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.100
If you wake up
during REM sleep,

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:42.210
you almost always
will remember a dream.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:43.713
Some of us don't
remember anything

00:07:43.713 --> 00:07:44.770
at all about our dreams,

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:46.490
and it doesn't
seem to harm them.

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:48.680
But it's not a true thing

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.580
that just because you don't
remember your dreams,

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:51.688
that you're not
having good sleep.

00:07:51.688 --> 00:07:53.020
What tells you you're
having good sleep

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.257
is how you feel
the next day.

00:07:54.257 --> 00:07:55.710
Robbins: Now, if you're
waking up with nightmares,

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:57.290
that could be a simple sign

00:07:57.290 --> 00:07:59.150
that maybe your
bedroom is too hot

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:00.760
and you need to turn
down the temperature.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:03.360
'Cause a hot bedroom
environment unfortunately

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:04.830
can create fragmented sleep

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.890
and cause you to wake up
often from nightmares.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:11.430
Now, "Eating cheese (or
other food) before bed

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:12.809
causes nightmares."

00:08:12.809 --> 00:08:16.180
Rapoport: I don't think I'm
aware of any particular food

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:17.910
that will do that
to everybody,

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.870
but it's very clear
that being uncomfortable

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:23.870
will precipitate both
bad sleep and waking up

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:25.300
and maybe even nightmares.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:28.910
So, imagine somebody with
irritable bowel syndrome

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:31.160
who knows that whenever
they eat, whatever,

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:35.280
gluten or some
specific food, spicy foods,

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.340
it upsets their stomach
when they're awake.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:38.173
Well, guess what?

00:08:38.173 --> 00:08:39.930
If they eat it before
they go to sleep,

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:41.071
it'll upset their sleep,

00:08:41.071 --> 00:08:42.950
and it may show up
as a nightmare.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:44.474
Robbins: "Loud snoring
is annoying

00:08:44.474 --> 00:08:45.890
but mostly harmless."

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:49.020
Rapoport: Loud snoring
is actually a sign

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:51.450
that there is a blockage
in your throat.

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:52.820
The mildest form
of blockage

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:54.970
just causes vibration, noise.

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:58.080
If you've ever played
with a piece of grass

00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:59.010
and you blow through it,

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:01.220
you know that if you
blow through a tube

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:03.700
or a structure that
can vibrate,

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:05.900
it starts to vibrate
and make noise.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:08.865
Many instruments are based
on exactly that principle.

00:09:08.865 --> 00:09:10.080
You're creating a vibration

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.803
by blowing through a
partially blocked tube.

00:09:13.803 --> 00:09:15.590
So snoring is just that.

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:17.150
And if that's all it was,

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:19.310
it wouldn't be all
that bad for you.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:23.160
But, unfortunately, it
usually isn't just by itself.

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.570
And especially when, as
they say on the question,

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:27.150
loud snoring.

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:30.140
That kind of snoring, and
especially if it's associated

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:32.924
with gasps and
snorts and pauses,

00:09:32.924 --> 00:09:35.450
is actually a sign of
a very common disorder

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:36.960
called sleep apnea.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:38.960
And sleep apnea is
when that blockage

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:41.770
gets a little bit worse
than just causing vibration

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:44.234
and actually blocks
the flow of air in.

00:09:44.234 --> 00:09:47.300
And when that happens,
you're actually choking.

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:48.838
And when that
gets complete,

00:09:48.838 --> 00:09:50.010
we call it an apnea,

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:51.940
"without breathing,"
from the Greek.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:54.570
And your body defends
itself against this blockage

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:55.745
by waking up, 
'cause everything

00:09:55.745 --> 00:09:57.213
gets normal when
you wake up.

00:09:57.213 --> 00:09:59.097
The trouble is that then
you go back to sleep

00:09:59.097 --> 00:10:00.270
and it happens again,

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:03.240
and it can happen
every 30 to 60 seconds.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:04.454
Robbins: "Hitting
snooze bar

00:10:04.454 --> 00:10:06.050
is better than
getting up."

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:07.724
We often hear that people

00:10:07.724 --> 00:10:10.280
have two, three,
four, five alarms

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:12.320
set up before they
get up in the morning.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:14.430
Now, the best thing
for all of us to do

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:16.521
would actually be to
practice sleep hygiene

00:10:16.521 --> 00:10:18.470
and have a
consistent bedtime

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:20.050
and actually wake up
with an alarm.

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:23.160
But of course that's a
lot harder in actuality.

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.150
The best thing for us,
by and large,

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:26.680
is to set your alarm clock

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:28.980
for the latest time that
you can in the morning

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:30.740
to allow for as much
sleep as possible

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:33.760
but that will allow you to
go about your daily routine

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:35.085
and get to work on time.

00:10:35.085 --> 00:10:37.790
Because if we're hitting
several snooze bars

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:39.900
and waiting, I believe
it's nine minutes,

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:41.620
and then another
nine minutes,

00:10:41.620 --> 00:10:46.010
all of that incremental
sleep is very rarely that.

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:47.440
It's much more fragmented.

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:49.332
It's very light
sleep, if anything.

00:10:49.332 --> 00:10:51.610
And the majority of REM
sleep is in the morning

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:52.702
right before we wake up.

00:10:52.702 --> 00:10:55.110
So try to protect
that as best you can

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.340
and set your alarm clock for
the latest possible time.

00:10:57.340 --> 00:10:59.832
"You can simply become
a morning person."

00:10:59.832 --> 00:11:01.373
Rapoport: So, the
difference between

00:11:01.373 --> 00:11:03.131
a morning and
an evening person

00:11:03.131 --> 00:11:05.860
appears to be influenced
by lots of things

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:07.317
that are probably genetic.

00:11:07.317 --> 00:11:11.560
And it's not something you
can just change by training.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:14.360
What you can do is
trick your biology

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:17.730
into thinking that you
live in Chicago

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:19.580
but you work in New York.

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:21.331
And that's what
blue light does.

00:11:21.331 --> 00:11:23.980
Robbins: With some
blue-light therapy, actually,

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:26.920
using bright-blue light
in the morning

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:30.063
can help shift those
true evening people

00:11:30.063 --> 00:11:31.107
a little bit earlier.

00:11:31.107 --> 00:11:33.280
Rapoport: It
basically tells you

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:35.070
that you're actually in
a different time zone

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:38.040
from where you are, and that
shifts you a little bit.

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:39.182
So that's the approach

00:11:39.182 --> 00:11:41.440
we use when the
problem is severe

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:42.710
and there's a need for that.

00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:44.580
It's actually shifting
when morning is

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.657
rather than shifting whether
you work in the morning.

00:11:47.657 --> 00:11:49.610
Robbins: So, "You can
catch up on sleep

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:52.000
by sleeping in
on the weekends."

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.003
Now, for the vast
majority of us,

00:11:54.003 --> 00:11:56.299
this is a very
common practice,

00:11:56.299 --> 00:11:57.610
this kind of sleeping in,

00:11:57.610 --> 00:11:59.293
and unfortunately,
in our society,

00:11:59.293 --> 00:12:01.467
we term it as this luxury,

00:12:01.467 --> 00:12:03.039
you know, "Oh, to sleep in."

00:12:03.039 --> 00:12:04.477
And that's because
most of us aren't

00:12:04.477 --> 00:12:06.060
getting enough sleep
during the work week

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:09.279
or adding bricks into our
backpack of sleep debt.

00:12:09.279 --> 00:12:10.963
Now, what sleeping in does

00:12:10.963 --> 00:12:13.850
is it sends a cue to
our circadian rhythm

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:15.510
that we're trying to
change time zones.

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:18.640
So if we extend our rising
time by more than an hour,

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.770
two, three, worse, four
hours into the morning,

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:24.170
you might feel better
than if you got up early,

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:27.035
but that sleep the next night
is gonna be compromised.

00:12:27.035 --> 00:12:29.010
Why? We call this
social jet lag.

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:30.600
Our body is trying to adapt.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:33.180
If you're a New
Yorker, physiologically,

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:35.000
your body thinks
you're in London

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:36.480
and you're trying to
adjust to that time,

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:38.170
so you're gonna be
fighting your physiology

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:39.876
come bedtime that
next night.

00:12:39.876 --> 00:12:41.380
So the best practice is

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:43.250
to keep a consistent
bedtime schedule

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:45.350
and try to get as much
sleep as you can.

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:48.030
Now, if you do have an
excessive sleep debt

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.460
and you really need to pay
that back on the weekends,

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:52.055
the best way
without interrupting

00:12:52.055 --> 00:12:52.970
your circadian rhythm

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:55.370
would be to do that with
a nap in the afternoon

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:56.580
because that's not
gonna change

00:12:56.580 --> 00:13:00.520
your body's physiological
circadian rhythm.

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:01.731
"Boredom makes you tired

00:13:01.731 --> 00:13:03.510
even if you got
enough sleep."

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:08.470
Now, yes, it is very true that
a boring meeting or lecture,

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:11.280
especially in the
afternoon, may be soporific.

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:12.809
But if you're in that
environment

00:13:12.809 --> 00:13:13.620
 and sleep-deprived,

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:14.650
it is a bellwether sign

00:13:14.650 --> 00:13:16.240
that you're not
getting enough sleep.

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:19.877
So, when people say, "The
airplane makes me tired.

00:13:19.877 --> 00:13:21.810
I get in the plane,
I fall right asleep."

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:26.391
Boredom alone, of course, is
not a sleep-inducing state.

00:13:26.391 --> 00:13:28.525
Rapoport: Boredom
is a way of

00:13:28.525 --> 00:13:30.560
unmasking your
sleep tendency.

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.150
We, in fact, use
that in testing.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:35.380
We put people in
boring situations

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:37.890
and see how long it takes
them to fall asleep.

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:40.970
If they are fully
satisfying their sleep need,

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:43.290
they don't fall asleep
for at least 20 minutes.

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:45.410
Robbins: Sleep is so
critical to our health

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:47.300
and our wellness
and our well-being,

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:48.510
and every night
does count.

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:50.360
In light of all the things
that we've talked about,

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:52.760
remember that if it's
not broken, don't fix it.

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:55.260
So do try to implement
some of the strategies

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:57.090
or put some of the
strategies to work

00:13:57.090 --> 00:13:58.500
that we've talked
about today

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:00.880
if you find that you
have a problem,

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:02.680
because at the end of
the day, small changes

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:04.250
do make a really
big difference,

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:06.150
especially when it
comes to our sleep.

